String in python. 
A string in python is a sequence of characters used to store text. anything from a single letter to a complete paragraph 
can be a string.

Examples:

Code:

name="Naman Nirwal"
langauge='Python'
message="I am learning Python"
number="12345"

Notice that "12345" is a string. Because it is enclosed in quotation marks, even though it contains digits.
We will learn strings in stages, from the basic to advanced concepts useful for DSA, coding interviews, and real-world
python development.

LEVEL - 1 STRING FUNDAMENTALS

1. Creating Strings
Python supports three common ways to write strings.

Code:

a='hello'
b="hello"
c="""hello
Welcome to Python"""

Single Quotes: 'Hello'
Double Quotes: "Hello"
Triple Quotes: """Hello""", it is useful for multiline strings.

We can either use single or double quotes for ordinary strings.

Code:

message="""Hello
this is Naman Nirwal"""
print(message)

Output:

Hello
this is Naman Nirwal

2. Printing Strings

name="Naman Nirwal"
print(name)
print("Hello Python")
print("my name is", name)

Output:

Naman Nirwal
Hello Python
my name is Naman Nirwal

3. String length: len()

VERY IMPORTANT --> The len() function returns the number of characters in a string, including spaces and punctuation.

Code:

text="Python"
print(len(text))

Output:

6

Code:

text="Hello World"
print(len(text))

Output:

11

VERY important --> The space between hello and world counts as one character.

4. Indexing strings 

Each character has an index, starting from zero.

Example: Code:

text="PYTHON"

 P Y T H O N
 0 1 2 3 4 5 

Each character's index is its position, starting at zero.

Code:

text="PYTHON"
print(text[0])
print(text[1])
print(text[3])
print(text[5])

Output:

P
Y
H
N

IMportant --> Accessing an index outside the string's valid range raise an IndexError.

5. Negative Indexing 

Python also lets you access characters from the end.

Code:

text="PYTHON"
print(text[-1])
print(text[-2])
print(text[-3])

Output:

N
O
H

The last character has an index -1, the second last index -2, and so on.

6. Slicing Strings

Slicing extracts a part of the String.

Syntax:

Code:

string[start:end:step]

About the start, stop, and step:

Start: index where extraction begins.
Stop: index where extraction ends, not included.
Step: how many positions to move each time.

Code:

name="Naman Nirwal"
print([::0])

Output:

this raises the ValueError

Code:

name="Naman Nirwal"
print([::1])

Output:

Naman Nirwal #default is 1

Code:

text="PYTHON"
print(text[0:3])
print(text[2:5])
print(text[:4])
print(text[2:])
print(text[::2])
print(text[::-1])
print(text[::-2])

Output:

PYT
THO
PYTH
THON
PTO
NOHTYP
NHY

IMPORTANT --> The second last example reverses the string using a slice with a step of -1.

7. strings are immutable

This is one of the most important concepts in python.

Immutable means we cannot change individual characters of an existing strings.

Code:

text="PYTHON"
text[0]="p" #this will create an issue known as type error

To produce "JYTHON", create a new string.

Code:

text="Python"
text="J"+text[1:]
print(text)

output:

Jython

The original string was not modified character by character. a new string was created and assigned to the text.

