LEVEL 5. Intermediate string concepts

23. Escape characters 

Escape sequences let you represent special characters inside strings.

sequence                         Meaning
\n                                  New Line
\t                                  tab
\\                                  backward slash
\'                                  single quote
\"                                  double quote

Code:

text="Hello\nPython"
print(text)

Output:

Hello
Python

A tab example:

Code:

text="Hello\tPython"
print(text)

Hello  Python

We can also use triple quoted strings for multiline text.

24. Raw Strings

A raw string, written with an r prefix, treats more blackslashes as literal characters rather than escape 
sequences. 

Code:

path=r"C:\new\test"
print(path) #C:\new\test

This is useful for writing windows paths and regular expressions patterns. Raw strings still follow Python's 
quoting rules. In particular, a raw string cannot end with an odd number of backslashes.

25. checking prefixes and suffixes 

use startswith() and endswith() to check the beginning or end of the string.

Code:

filename="report.pdf"
print(filename.startswith("report")) #True
print(filename.endswith("pdf")) #True
print(filename.endswith(".txt")) #False

VERY IMPORTANT --> We can also pass a tuple of possible suffixes.

Code:

filename="report.txt"
print(filename.endswith((".txt",".pdf"))

Output:

True

This will return True as one index's value matches the .txt.

26. string alignment 

python can align strings within a specified width.

Code:

text="Python"
print(text.center(12,"-"))
print(text.ljust(12,"-"))
print(text.rjust(12,"-"))

Output:

---Python---
Python------
------Python

about the: center(), ljust(), rjust().

center(): centers the string.
ljust(): aligns it to the left.
rjust(): aligns it to the right.

27. string immutability and memory

Code:

text="Hello"
text=text+"Hello"

output:

HelloHello

python does not extend the existing string in place. the concatenation produces a new string, and the variable
text is reassigned to it.

This matters when repeatedly building large strings.

Less efficient for many pieces:

Code:

result=""
for word in ["python", "is", "powerful"]:
    result+=word+" "
print(result)

Output:

python is powerful

a common alternative is:

Code:

words=["python","is","powerful"]
result=" ".join(words)
print(result)

Output:

python is powerful

Using join() is generally preferable when combining many strings from a collection.

























